---
import AlgoPagina from "../../components/AlgoPagina.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CorridaModos } from "../../components/CorridaLab.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
---

<Base domain="algorithms" title="Sorting, swap by swap: eight algorithms, three classes — OpenLabs" description="Eight sorting algorithms in three classes — O(n²), O(n log n) and the non-comparison O(n) sorts — race on the same scattered bars with the same clock. Hear every comparison, follow the pseudocode line by line, and watch the Big-O curves grow underneath.">
<SiteNav current="labs" />
<AlgoPagina
  titulo="Sorting, swap by swap: eight algorithms, three classes"
  href="labs/sorting-race"
  nota={["Same array. Same clock.", "Race inside a class to see constants; across classes to see exponents.", "The pitch is the value being touched."]}
  quebre={[
    { b: "Set the input to “already sorted”.", texto: "Bubble and insertion finish almost instantly — one pass, no swaps — while quicksort, with its last-item pivot, does the full n(n−1)/2 comparisons. The fastest algorithm on random data is the slowest here." },
    { b: "Set it to “reversed”.", texto: "Insertion now swaps every pair: n(n−1)/2 swaps. Selection does exactly the same number of comparisons as on any other input — it never looks at what it has already learned." },
    { b: "Type 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3.", texto: "Nothing to do, and you can see who knows it: bubble stops after one pass; selection still scans everything." },
    { b: "Race the O(n + k) class against quicksort at 256 items.", texto: "Counting and radix sort never compare two items — the compare counter stays at zero. They beat the n log n barrier because the values are small whole numbers; that barrier only binds sorts that compare." },
    { b: "Pick “nearly sorted” and race insertion against heapsort.", texto: "Insertion sort only pays for what is out of place and finishes far ahead; heapsort does its full n log n whatever the input." },
  ]}
  erros={[
    { crenca: "Quicksort is always the fastest.", verdade: "On average it is O(n log n) with small constants. With a naive pivot on sorted input it is O(n²) — the race shows it finishing last." },
    { crenca: "Bubble sort is useless.", verdade: "It is O(n²) on average, but on an almost-sorted array it is one pass: O(n). Insertion sort shares that, and real libraries use it for small or nearly sorted runs." },
    { crenca: "Fewer comparisons always means faster.", verdade: "Merge sort makes few comparisons but writes every item at every level, and needs n extra slots. The table counts swaps and writes separately so you can see the trade." },
    { crenca: "The algorithm decides the cost on its own.", verdade: "The input decides it as much. Every row of the podium can change by switching from random to sorted to reversed." },
  ]}
  fronteira="Every step costs the same here, which real hardware does not do: a write can cost more than a comparison, and memory access patterns (cache) matter. The quicksort uses the last item as pivot on purpose, to show its worst case; production quicksorts choose pivots more carefully and switch to insertion sort for small runs. Radix sort works on the decimal digits of small whole numbers; stability and parallel sorts are out of scope."
  ligacoes={[
    { href: "labs/how-work-grows", texto: "Six ways to grow — where O(n²) and O(n log n) come from" },
    { href: "labs/rpn", texto: "A stack machine — the stack that quicksort's recursion hides" },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Eight algorithms in three classes, one array, one clock. Every comparison, swap,
    write or read costs one step, and all lanes advance together — so the lane that
    finishes first is the one that did the least work on <i>this</i> input. Race
    algorithms of the same class and the difference is a constant; race across classes
    and it is the exponent, and it grows with every item you add.
  </p>
  <p>
    What separates them is not the operation. They all compare and move items. It is
    <b>who decides which pair to look at next</b>: the bubble always looks at the next
    neighbour; insertion slides one item left; selection scans for the minimum; merge
    takes the smaller head of two sorted halves; quicksort throws everything to one side
    of a pivot. Change that one rule and the cost goes from <i>n</i>² to <i>n</i> log <i>n</i>.
  </p>
  <CorridaModos slot="palco" client:only="react" />
  <Predicao
    slot="predicao"
    client:visible
    pergunta="The array is already sorted, 64 items. Which algorithm finishes LAST in the race?"
    opcoes={["Quicksort (last item as pivot)", "Bubble sort", "Merge sort"]}
    correta={0}
    revelacao="With the last item as pivot, every partition of a sorted array is as unbalanced as possible: one side empty, the other with everything else. That is 63 + 62 + … + 1 = 2 016 comparisons — the worst case. Bubble sort does one pass of 63 comparisons and stops."
  />
</AlgoPagina>
<SiteFooter />
</Base>
